# Upstream patch for github.com/cloudflare/azul — Ed25519 chain signatures in x509_util
#
# SUBMITTED UPSTREAM: https://github.com/cloudflare/azul/pull/281 (2026-08-28)
# Kept here so notme-ct can be built against a vendored fork if the PR stalls,
# and so the precondition is reviewable without leaving this repo.
#
# WHY: azul's x509_util::is_link_valid dispatches on the signature OID and
# accepts ECDSA P-256/384/521 and RSA PKCS#1 v1.5 only. Ed25519 (RFC 8410,
# id-Ed25519 1.3.101.112) falls to `_ => false`, so a Static CT log built on
# ct_worker refuses every Ed25519-signed chain at add-chain — as if the
# signature were invalid, not as an unsupported algorithm. notme's root and
# every certificate it issues are Ed25519.
#
# WHAT: one verify_ed25519 arm using ed25519_dalek (already a workspace
# dependency for cosignatures) + one Cargo line + an openssl-verified
# Ed25519 root/intermediate/leaf fixture chain + a test_validate_chain! case.
# verify_strict, not verify: it rejects non-canonical encodings and
# small-order keys, which matters for a log where signature malleability
# could mean two entries for one logical certificate.
#
# PROOF (re-run against a fresh clone of HEAD 2051772 before submitting):
#   cargo test -p x509_util                                      -> 29 passed
#   cargo clippy -p x509_util --all-targets -Dwarnings -Dpedantic -> clean
#   cargo fmt --all --check                                      -> clean
#   match arm removed -> ed25519_chain_success FAILS, nothing else moves
#
# Tracked by notme-1b46a8. Apply from an azul checkout with:
#   git apply docs/design/azul-ed25519.patch

From e6dda46e4adab30be4ff4f74ded0daf072ce10e9 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: jamestexas <18285880+jamestexas@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sat, 29 Aug 2026 14:26:16 -0600
Subject: [PATCH] x509_util: accept Ed25519 chain signatures (RFC 8410)
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

is_link_valid dispatches on the child certificate's signature algorithm OID
and handles ECDSA P-256/384/521 and RSA PKCS#1 v1.5. id-Ed25519
(1.3.101.112) falls through to `_ => false`, so a chain signed with Ed25519
is rejected as if the signature were invalid rather than as an unsupported
algorithm — and a Static CT log built on ct_worker refuses every such
submission at add-chain.

Ed25519 is a standard X.509 signature algorithm (RFC 8410) and is permitted
for both roots and subordinate CAs, so a log whose accepted roots include an
Ed25519 CA cannot currently log anything it issues.

Adds a verify_ed25519 arm alongside the existing ones, using ed25519-dalek
(already a workspace dependency for cosignatures). It uses verify_strict,
which rejects non-canonical encodings and small-order public keys — the
stricter choice, and the appropriate one where signature malleability could
mean two distinct log entries for one logical certificate.

Test fixtures are an openssl-generated root (pathlen:1) → intermediate
(pathlen:0) → leaf chain, confirmed with `openssl verify -CAfile root.pem
-untrusted int.pem leaf.pem` before being committed, so the fixtures are
validated by something other than the code under test. Removing the match
arm fails ed25519_chain_success and no other test.

cargo test -p x509_util: 29 passed. cargo clippy -p x509_util --all-targets
-- -Dwarnings -Dclippy::pedantic: clean. cargo fmt --all --check: clean.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Signed-off-by: jamestexas <18285880+jamestexas@users.noreply.github.com>
